# Line endings, decided here rather than by each person's Git install.
#
# Without this file, every clone's line endings depend on a checkbox someone
# ticked when they installed Git for Windows and has long forgotten. The
# default is usually right, which is the problem: it is right until it is not,
# and the failure it produces points nowhere near its cause.
#
# `eol=lf` is stronger than normalising on commit: it keeps LF in the working
# copy too, so what your editor shows is what the repository holds. The case
# that matters is `.github/workflows/`, where a `run:` block is a bash script
# executed on a Linux runner. A workflow committed with CRLF fails there with
# `$'\r': command not found`, and nothing in that message mentions Windows,
# Git, or line endings.
#
# Having set this, run `git add --renormalize .` once so anything already
# committed with the wrong endings is restaged with the right ones.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.ipynb text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
